what i had meant for you to do is replicate what the function does, rather than the function itself. making new functions can work, but isnt needed. Within startAction(), you already have variables to easily access the subject and the action, so easy options are to:
- use action.applyItemUserEffect(). even though theres a target parameter, it actually isnt used and can be ignored
Spoiler
Code:
BattleManager.startAction = function() {
    const subject = this._subject;
    const action = subject.currentAction();
    const targets = action.makeTargets();
    this._phase = "action";
    this._action = action;
    this._targets = targets;
    subject.cancelMotionRefresh();
    subject.useItem(action.item());
    action.applyItemUserEffect();   //added line
    this._action.applyGlobal();
    this._logWindow.startAction(subject, action, targets);
};
- or to use subject.gainTp(). You'd have to adapt the math in applyItemUserEffect to determine how much should be gained, but you have all the parts.
