Robro33,
Thank you again for the replies! Digging into your suggestions now.
I typed Game_Action.prototype.applyItemUserEffect into the console and got the following:
Looks like Visustella is doing something with it, but it has been obfuscated.
However, I took the code you wrote in the spoiler tag, put it into the plugin for this, and commented out the rest of the stuff I had in the plugin including my attempt at the alias and it all works perfectly! There is no error or crash and each character is gaining only 10 TP on using an AoE spell instead of 10 for every target (10 being the number entered into the "TP Gain:" box.
I'm going to try turning this into an alias and see if everything goes smoothly, as it does appear that Visustella may have changed the function and is using it for something, likely in the battle core, and I don't want to burn whatever it is they're already cooking.
I will edit this post with the results shortly if I'm able to get it working!
Edit:
Okay, I'm getting the same error as before. I'm starting to think it's something I'm doing wrong in the alias that is causing this, I'm going to look into that now. I know the code without being alias'd works in a brand new project with no other plugins, and also just straight up works without being alias'd in my actual project, so it must be the aliasing that is causing the issue?
Here is the error report:
This is what I wrote for the alias'd code:
NinGainTPPlugin = Game_Action.prototype.applyItemUserEffect
Game_Action.prototype.applyItemUserEffect = function(target) {
NinGainTPPlugin.call(this);
if (!this._tpGained){
var value = Math.floor(this.item().tpGain * this.subject().tcr);
this.subject().gainSilentTp(value);
this._tpGained = true;
}
};
Edit 2:
Okay, it was indeed my alias that was wrote incorrectly. I needed NinGainTPPlugin.call(this, target, and possibly the const at the beginning). The following alias works just fine:
const NinGainTPPlugin = Game_Action.prototype.applyItemUserEffect
Game_Action.prototype.applyItemUserEffect = function(target) {
NinGainTPPlugin.call(this, target);
if (!this._tpGained){
var value = Math.floor(this.item().tpGain * this.subject().tcr);
this.subject().gainSilentTp(value);
this._tpGained = true;
}
};
However, because this is
adding onto
the existing function, it's not working as intended. Now characters get 10 TP for each target AND 10 additional TP, haha.
So I think I will have to do an actual overwrite in order to get this work as I want it too, and just see if anything else gets messed up through some playtesting.
